#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""buildqd: the daemon behind buildq, a load-aware build queue for one shared Linux machine.

Callers drop off a build and get a ticket, a UUID:

    buildq submit -- cargo test -p api      # prints the ticket and returns at once
    buildq status <ticket>                  # queued (and why), running (and how big), or finished
    buildq wait <ticket>                    # blocks until it ends; exits with its exit code
    buildq run -- cargo test -p api         # submit, stream the output, exit with its exit code

The daemon holds each ticket until the machine has room for its build, judged from what the
machine is doing right now rather than from a fixed number of slots:

  Memory  The build's expected peak must fit in MemAvailable, less what every running build may
          still grow into (its expected peak minus what it holds now), less mem_floor. Never
          skipped. No build starts while memory pressure (PSI "full") is above mem_psi_block.
  CPU     The build's minimum cores must fit in what is free: cpu_target x cores, less the busy
          cores measured machine-wide (CI runners, containers and other users included), less the
          cores that builds started in the last ramp_seconds haven't reached yet, plus the cores
          that resizable builds could give back. Skipped when no build is running, so outside
          load can slow the queue but never stall it.
  Other   At most max_jobs builds; no build while its filesystem has less than disk_floor free;
          one build at a time per workspace in an exclusive lane.

Each build gets a number of cores between its lane's min_cores and what it can use, its "want",
learned from earlier runs: a run that used every core it was given wants more next time, a run
that left cores idle wants fewer. The number goes into the build's environment (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS,
RUST_TEST_THREADS, MAKEFLAGS -jN, CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL, GOMAXPROCS, BUILDQ_CORES) unless the
caller set those. Builds whose tools speak the GNU make jobserver protocol (cargo, make, ninja)
also get a jobserver of their own, which the daemon resizes every few seconds: they grow into idle
cores and give cores back when other builds need them.

Each build runs as a transient systemd user service, buildq-<ticket>.service in buildq.slice, with
its lane's properties (MemoryHigh, MemoryMax, ...). Inside it, `buildqd --supervise <ticket dir>`
runs the command with the caller's environment and working directory, copies its output to the
ticket's output.log, enforces its timeout, stops whatever it leaves behind, and records its exit
status. Builds don't depend on the caller or the daemon: the daemon can restart while they run and
picks them up again from the ticket files.

See README.md and docs/design.md.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import asyncio
